<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Inherited (helper) entitlements for Olympus.

  electron-builder's `mac.entitlementsInherit` points here. macOS applies this
  file to every Chrome Helper process Electron spawns: the GPU process, the
  network service, the renderer processes, and the utility processes.

  The important difference from the main app's entitlements is `disable-library-
  validation`. Electron's helper processes load the Chromium framework
  dylibs and, in a packaged app, they load them from a path that is not
  signed by the same identity as the outer bundle. Under hardened runtime the
  kernel refuses to load a dylib signed by a different team, and every helper
  dies with SIGKILL — which presents to the user as "the app opens a window and
  then goes blank". This key is the documented, Apple-sanctioned exception for
  exactly that situation, and it is required for Electron's multi-process model
  to work at all under the hardened runtime.

  Everything else is deliberately absent for the same reasons given in
  entitlements.mac.plist: no camera, no microphone, no location, and no
  App Sandbox network keys (loopback-only traffic needs no entitlement, and
  Olympus is not sandboxed because it must spawn processes and touch the
  user's filesystem).

  If you add native Node addons later, you will also need
  com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables only for development
  builds; never ship that.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
